It looks like the tooling to generate SR classes changed from how it used to be in buildtools.

CoreRT repo still builds with buildtools. The generated SR classes look like this:

```csharp
    internal static partial class SR
    {

#if !DEBUGRESOURCES
        // SNIP

        internal static string Argument_OffsetOfFieldNotFound {
            get { return System.SR.GetResourceString("Argument_OffsetOfFieldNotFound", null); }
        }
#else
        // SNIP

        internal static string Argument_OffsetOfFieldNotFound {
            get { return System.SR.GetResourceString("Argument_OffsetOfFieldNotFound", @"Field passed in is not a marshaled member of the type '{0}'."); }
        }
#endif
    }
```

Whereas the arcade tooling generates:

```csharp
    internal static partial class SR
    {
        /// <summary>Compile instantiations from reference modules used in the current module</summary>
        internal static string @BubbleGenericsOption => GetResourceString("BubbleGenericsOption");
```

I don't know about the debugger comment either.

I looked at the difference between the corelib and the rest of the libraries, in corelib, we have GetString implemented as @vitek-karas mentioned 

```C#
	internal static string GetResourceString(string resourceKey)
	{
		return GetResourceString(resourceKey, string.Empty);
	}
```

but in the rest of the libraries, we have the method defined with the default parameter 

```C#
	internal static string GetResourceString(string resourceKey, string defaultString = null)
	{
		if (UsingResourceKeys())
		{
		}
		string text = null;
		try
		{
			text = ResourceManager.GetString(resourceKey);
		}
		catch (MissingManifestResourceException)
		{
		}
		if (defaultString != null && resourceKey.Equals(text))
		{
			return defaultString;
		}
		return text;
	}
```
https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/fc15e38da07e26dc2790bf067bd9d7ec17cf44e9/src/libraries/Common/src/System/SR.cs#L22

So it looks the issue is specific to corelib. @vitek-karas may confirm if seeing the same with any other library. 

Another point, the code difference between the corelib and other libraries was originally introduced because corelib was not including the ResourceManager code in the first place (in .NET Native). but this is not the case anymore as corelib now carries the ResourceManager code. I believe this code can be unified now by removing the SR.cs file from corelib and let it link to the SR.cs which placed under the common source.

I don't recall anything about the debugger integration comment, but I'll try to search more to know if I can find anything that can help there.
